{"title":"Career Roadmap — Corrosion Engineer","role":"Corrosion Engineer","summary":"A Corrosion Engineer prevents, controls and investigates material degradation caused by chemical, electrochemical, environmental and mechanical conditions. The role is important across oil & gas, petrochemicals, power plants, marine infrastructure, pipelines, chemical plants, refineries, aerospace and infrastructure.","facts":{},"about":[{"label":"Direct alignment","text":"Very High — Metallurgical & Materials Engineering provides the strongest foundation because corrosion is closely connected to materials science, metallurgy, electrochemistry, surface engineering and failure analysis."}],"intro":[],"horizons":[],"sections":[{"heading":"Career Horizons","kind":"horizons","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["Period","Career focus"],"rows":[[{"text":"2026–2030"},{"text":"Metallurgy, materials science, electrochemistry, corrosion mechanisms and laboratory testing"}],[{"text":"2030–2035"},{"text":"Corrosion Engineer → Materials/Integrity Engineer → Senior Corrosion Engineer"}],[{"text":"2035–2040"},{"text":"Corrosion Specialist, Materials Integrity Engineer, Inspection/Integrity Manager"}],[{"text":"2040–2046"},{"text":"Principal Corrosion Engineer, Asset Integrity Manager, Corrosion & Materials Head or Technical Consultant"}]]}]},{"heading":"What to Build Each Year","kind":"build","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["Year","Core learning","Practical outcome"],"rows":[[{"text":"Year 1"},{"text":"Chemistry, physics, mathematics, engineering fundamentals"},{"text":"Understand materials and chemical environments"}],[{"text":"Year 2"},{"text":"Metallurgy, materials science, thermodynamics, electrochemistry"},{"text":"Understand why materials corrode"}],[{"text":"Year 3"},{"text":"Corrosion engineering, coatings, cathodic protection, failure analysis"},{"text":"Perform corrosion assessment"}],[{"text":"Year 4"},{"text":"Corrosion control, materials selection, inspection, asset integrity"},{